perl.h: a pointer wider than every integer gets a PTRV that holds its address, and the data/function casts stop going through one.

Two defects on a target whose pointer is a 16-byte capability with an 8-byte
address, both silent:

PTRV picks the first of UV, unsigned long, and `unsigned` whose size equals
PTRSIZE. At PTRSIZE 16 none matches and the fallthrough takes `unsigned`, so
every PTR2UV, PTR2IV and PTR2nat truncates an address to 32 bits, with no
warning. PTRV now takes UV where a pointer is wider than a UV: the widest
integer that holds the address.

DPTR2FPTR and FPTR2DPTR convert between data and function pointers through
PTR2nat, which is legal ISO C and loses a capability's authority. The call
through the recovered pointer traps: "cs.cjalr requires capability in rs1" in
Perl_filter_read, which every `perl -e` reaches (measured 2026-09-26). Both
become direct casts where a pointer is wider than a UV -- outside ISO C for the
reason perl's own comment gives, and what every POSIX dlsym() caller relies on.
39 sites use the pair.

Neither condition can hold on a platform with an integer as wide as its
pointers, so nothing changes there.

This one is generated against perl.h AFTER perl-cross's own xconfig.patch,
which the build script applies first (its `crosspatch` target): both touch
perl.h, and applied the other way round each makes the other look
already-applied.

--- a/perl.h	2026-09-26 15:17:07.593919336 +0800
+++ b/perl.h	2026-09-26 15:17:07.602185620 +0800
@@ -2189,6 +2189,17 @@
 #elif PTRSIZE == LONGSIZE
 #  define PTRV			unsigned long
 #  define PTR2ul(p)		(unsigned long)(p)
+#elif PTRSIZE > UVSIZE
+/* capstone: a pointer wider than any integer -- a 16-byte capability whose
+   ADDRESS is 8 bytes. The fallthrough below picks `unsigned`, which truncates
+   every PTR2UV, PTR2IV and PTR2nat to 32 bits silently; PTRV is the widest
+   integer that holds the address instead. That is enough for the one-way uses
+   (printing, hashing, an address compared with another address) and it cannot be
+   enough for a round trip: an integer carries no authority, so a pointer that
+   goes through one comes back unusable. DPTR2FPTR and FPTR2DPTR below are the
+   round trips perl makes on this path, and they stop going through an integer. */
+#  define PTRV			UV
+#  define PTR2ul(p)		(unsigned long)(p)
 #else
 #  define PTRV			unsigned
 #endif
@@ -2225,8 +2236,19 @@
  * The only feasible use is probably temporarily storing
  * function pointers in a data pointer (such as a void pointer). */
 
+#if PTRSIZE > UVSIZE
+/* capstone: the two casts the comment above describes would pass the pointer
+   through an integer, which drops a capability's authority: the call through the
+   recovered function pointer traps ("cs.cjalr requires capability in rs1", in
+   Perl_filter_read, on every `perl -e` -- measured 2026-09-26). A direct cast
+   keeps it. Casting between object and function pointers is outside ISO C for
+   the reason the comment gives, and is what every POSIX dlsym() caller does. */
+#  define DPTR2FPTR(t,p) ((t)(p))	/* data pointer to function pointer */
+#  define FPTR2DPTR(t,p) ((t)(p))	/* function pointer to data pointer */
+#else
 #define DPTR2FPTR(t,p) ((t)PTR2nat(p))	/* data pointer to function pointer */
 #define FPTR2DPTR(t,p) ((t)PTR2nat(p))	/* function pointer to data pointer */
+#endif
 
 #ifdef USE_LONG_DOUBLE
 #  if LONG_DOUBLESIZE == DOUBLESIZE
